		<dc:title id="title">The Trap</dc:title>
		<meta property="file-as" refines="#title">Trap, The</meta>
		<dc:subject id="subject-1">Autobiographical fiction</dc:subject>
		<meta property="authority" refines="#subject-1">LCSH</meta>
		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-1">sh85048066</meta>
		<dc:subject id="subject-2">Women--England--Fiction</dc:subject>
		<meta property="authority" refines="#subject-2">LCSH</meta>
		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-2">sh2008113501</meta>
		<dc:subject id="subject-3">England--Social life and customs--20th century--Fiction</dc:subject>
		<meta property="authority" refines="#subject-3">LCSH</meta>
		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-3">sh2008114942</meta>
		<meta property="schema:genre">Fiction</meta>
		<meta id="collection-1" property="belongs-to-collection">Pilgrimage</meta>
		<meta property="collection-type" refines="#collection-1">set</meta>
		<meta property="group-position" refines="#collection-1">8</meta>
		<meta property="schema:abstract">At the start of the 20th century an independent woman and budding writer takes an apartment with a spinster night-school teacher.</meta>
		<dc:description>
			&lt;p&gt;&lt;i&gt;The Trap&lt;/i&gt; is the eighth “chapter volume” of &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/dorothy-m-richardson"&gt;Dorothy M. Richardson’s&lt;/a&gt; pioneering semi-autobiographical series, &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/collections/pilgrimage"&gt;Pilgrimage&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;Series protagonist Miriam Henderson impulsively takes a flat in Flaxman Court with the very prim Selina Holland. Miriam struggles to introduce a disapproving Miss Holland to her bohemian London. Their apartment is across the way from that of a famous poet, E. W. Sayce—and Miss Holland is not impressed. (In later editions, Sayce is instead identified by Richardson as &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/w-b-yeats"&gt;W. B. Yeats&lt;/a&gt;.)&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;As is typical in the Pilgrimage volumes, this is a story in which nothing much “happens”—Richardson herself dismissed the notion of plot as “lollipops for children”—but which paints a vivid and intimate portrait of a young writer’s mind, and brings to life the world that shaped her.&lt;/p&gt;
		</dc:description>
